11.m:
"May Iḷā, the mother of the herd, together with the rivers, or Urvaśī, praise us;
or Urvaśī, mighty in heaven, being praised, covering over the mortal's oblation;
may she attend upon us for the nourishment of strength." —
May Iḷā praise us, the mother of the herd, the mother of all, together with (smad) the rivers. Or may Urvaśī praise us; or Urvaśī, mighty in heaven (bṛhaddivā), being praised, covering over the oblation of the mortal—of the wandering man, or of light, or of water; may she attend upon us for the nourishment of food.
Rodasī is the wife of Rudra. Regarding her there is this [verse]:

12.m:
"This Viṣṇu strode across; thrice he set down his foot;
[all] is gathered in its dust."
Whatever exists here, that Viṣṇu strides across. Thrice he sets down his foot for a threefold existence: "on earth, in the intermediate region, in heaven," says Śākapūṇi;
"at the ascent, at the footstep of Viṣṇu, at Gayaśiras," says Aurṇavābha. "Gathered in its dust": in the swelling intermediate region his footstep is not seen. Or it may be in the sense of a comparison: "Gathered in its dust, his footstep is not seen, as if dusty." The dust particles (pāṃsavaḥ) are so called because they are produced (sūyante) by feet; or because they lie fallen (pannāḥ śerate); or because they are fit to be trodden upon (paṃsanīyāḥ).
